do $10B M&A. Its acquisitions (TrainingPeaks, TrainHeroic:\n  undisclosed, but small) are tuck-ins. Base rate not binding [Estimate]\n- 5-yr survival: \u224895% given $4.1B net cash and 0 net debt [Estimate]\n\nSTEP 1.5-2. Deviation from Base Rate\n  My 5-yr bull case assumes ~9% revenue CAGR. Base rate for a company this\n  size (GD 2025: $7.25B \u2192 $11B by 2031) is ~10\u201312% top-line CAGR. I am\n  deviating \u22122 to \u22123 pts below that \u2014 defensible given:\n    1. Outdoor segment declined \u22125% in H1 2026 (already a deceleration event);\n    2. Auto OEM mature (rebirth only with new OEM programs, not in 2026);\n    3. Apple Watch Ultra/SE competition caps premium fitness share.\n  I am NOT deviating above base rates, so no red-flag (>2.0\u00d7) trigger fires.\n\nSTEP 1.5-3. Base Rate Shock Check: PASS.\n  Success probability implied by my bull case (25% weight) is within base rates.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\nSECTION 2 \u2014 BUSINESS QUALITY\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\n\nSTEP 2-1. Business Understanding\n  Garmin designs, manufactures and sells 5 verticals: fitness wearables,\n  outdoor GPS, aviation avionics, marine electronics, and automotive OEM.\n  It is vertically integrated (in-house design, manufacturing, software,\n  distribution), which gives it gross margins near 60% despite hardware\n  competition. The company monetizes an installed base via premium devices,\n  subscription services (Connect+, inReach), and aviation database updates.\n  Understandability: HIGH.\n\nSTEP 2-2. Moat Assessment (0-3 each) + Direction\n  Intangible assets (brand, patents):        3  \u2014 top-2 brand in fitness GPS;\n                                               11\u00d7 Boeing/Embraer supplier award\n  Switching costs:                           3  \u2014 aviation certification, marine\n                                               installs, Connect data history\n  Network effects:                           2  \u2014 Connect challenges, family\n                                               accounts; weaker than social apps\n  Cost advantage:                            2  \u2014 vertical integration + scale;\n                                               not the cheapest manufacturer\n  Economies of scale:                        2  \u2014 component procurement, R&D\n                                               spread over 5 segments\n  Regulatory barriers:                       3  \u2014 aviation/marine certifications\n  Data moat:                                 2  \u2014 years of health/location data;\n                                               weaker outside fitness vertical\n  TOTAL: 17/21 \u2192 FORTRESS [Assumption]\n  Moat direction: slightly WIDENING in fitness (share gains vs Apple/Fitbit),\n  STABLE in aviation/marine, slightly NARROWING in outdoor (auto/adventure\n  watch softness Q2 2026, [Actual]).\n\nSTEP 2-3. Lynch Classification: FAST GROWER (mature).\n  Revenue CAGR 11.6% trailing 5-yr [Arithmetic]; earnings CAGR \u2248 high-teens;\n  but 5-segment diversification lowers single-line risk. Checkpoints:\n  - Inventory: $1.772B FY2025, \u0394 +20% YoY \u2014 ABOVE revenue growth, FLAG;\n    monitor sell-through [Actual]\n  - New products: CIRQA smart band, AXIS flight displays, Forerunner 70/170 \u2014\n    strong cadence H2 2026 [Actual]\n  - Insider selling: no 6-mo cluster captured [Unconfirmed]\n\nSTEP 2-4. Financial Strength Scorecard\n  Revenue growth (5-yr CAGR): 11.6% [Arithmetic] \u2014 EXCELLENT\n  Op margin 12-yr median: 23.6%; latest 4Q: ~25.5% [Arithmetic] \u2014 EXCELLENT\n  EPS growth: +21.7% YoY Q1 2026 [Actual]; +21.3% Q4 2025 [Actual]\n  ROE: 21.9% [Aggregator]; ROIC calc \u2248 38% (pre-intangibles adj.) [Estimate]\n  FCF margin: 1.452/7.464 = 19.5% TTM [Arithmetic]\n  FCF conversion: 1.452/1.749 = 83% TTM [Arithmetic]\n  Net cash: ~$4.14B (cash + ST/LT investments \u2212 reported LTD) [Estimate]\n  Interest coverage: effectively infinite (no LTD) [Estimate]\n  Judgment: EXCELLENT.\n\nSTEP 2-5. Capital Allocation Scorecard (total /15)\n  1. Buyback efficiency: 2/3 \u2014 $500M program at ~$260\u2013300, modest size vs capex;\n     no peak-dilution abuse; shares \u22120.0%/yr 3-yr CAGR [Actual]\n  2. M&A track record:    2/3 \u2014 TrainingPeaks/TrainHeroic logical; no large\n     impairments disclosed [Actual]\n  3. Capex efficiency:    3/3 \u2014 capital-light ($0.27B vs $1.63B OCF 2025) with\n     strong incremental ROIC [Actual]\n  4. Reinvestment mix:    3/3 \u2014 dividends ($4.20/sh/yr) + buyback + R&D all\n     funded organically [Actual]\n  5. Guidance accuracy:   3/3 \u2014 raised FY26 guidance July 2026; consistent\n     history of hitting or beating [Actual]\n  Subtotal: 13/15. Insider ownership bonus +1 (14.69% [Aggregator])\n  Total: 14/15 \u2014 EXCELLENT.\n\n\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2550\u2